            <history xml:id="history_a6e49ff2">
               <eventList type="performances" xml:id="eventList_a2b77a7d">
                  <event xml:id="event_ba11d9ef">
                     <date isodate="1765-03-31" xml:id="date_f42bc243">1765-03-31</date>
                     <geogName role="venue" xml:id="geogName_5c3c19b8">Christiansborg Slotskirke</geogName>
                     <geogName role="place" xml:id="geogName_8207b6dd">Copenhagen</geogName>
                     <desc xml:id="desc_a1c7e316"/>
                     <biblList xml:id="biblList_5b15b88c">
                        <head xml:id="head_d1bc8c42">Reviews</head>
                        <bibl label="" xml:id="bibl_a98782c4">
                           <genre xml:id="genre_4059157f">article</genre>
                           <genre xml:id="genre_3ce077bd">journal</genre>
                           <title level="a" xml:id="title_0a4412ff"/>
                           <title level="j" xml:id="title_12693192"/>
                        </bibl>
                     </biblList>
                     <biblList xml:id="biblList_c81916a3">
                        <head xml:id="head_0ee93d9b">Evidence</head>
                     </biblList>
                  </event>
               </eventList>
               <p xml:id="idm129">In 1765 <persName xml:id="idm130" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Crown Prince Christian</persName>’s confirmation was to be celebrated and Scheibe composed the present cantata for which he received 100 Rigsdaler as a token. The event took place on Palm Sunday, on 31 March which was also Frederik V's birthday, to which the nobles could fetch a ticket and enter the church; also court employees had access. The examiner was the Bishop, <persName xml:id="idm131" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Ludvig Harboe</persName>, while the royal Chaplain, <persName xml:id="idm132" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Johann Andreas Cramer</persName>, held the sermons. Though the title page of the printed text, which is the only source to the work that has survived, neither indicates author nor composer, a critique of the work that appeared in the journal <rend xml:id="idm133" fontstyle="italic">Samling af adskillige Skrifter til de skiønne Videnskabers og det danske Sprogs Opkomst og Tarv</rend> (1765; SchW C1:010) led Scheibe to write a fifty-two page fierce response. It was published later that same year. The critique of the cantata, mentioning Cramer as the author of the text, says regarding Scheibe’s work: 'This excellent cantata deserved an excellent composition, and we admit with pleasure that the Scheibean [work] did not lack charm though we would also have liked if the Kapellmeister more often would let himself be animated by the genius of his poet and compose with more expression and less idleness in several of the best sections'. Scheibe's main problem with the review was that, though it indeed mentions the work as a masterpiece, it also criticised his work as being an imitation ('slet Parodie') of bad Italian composers – and even worse: he was criticised for his setting of the text or the art of the recitative ('Recitativens Kunst'). It was around this time that Scheibe wrote extensively on the art of setting music to text (SchW C1:003–005), conversing with both <persName xml:id="idm134" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Friedrich Gottlieb Klopstock</persName> and <persName xml:id="idm135" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Heinrich Wilhelm von Gerstenberg</persName> on the subject. His fierce response of 1765 also includes a detailed analysis of some of the sections. The cantata is also mentioned in the introduction to his<rend xml:id="idm136" fontstyle="italic"> Kleine Lieder für Kinder</rend>, 1768 (SchW B5:Coll.003). It seems that the discussion that followed Scheibes’ fierce response (SchW C1:010) included also foreign periodicals.</p>
            </history>
